        {"q": "What is depthwise separable convolution and why is it more efficient?", "a": "A convolution that processes depth differently on CPUs vs GPUs", "b": "It splits a standard convolution into depthwise (per-channel spatial convolution) + pointwise (1×1 across channels), reducing computation significantly", "c": "A convolution that only applies to the depth (Z) axis", "d": "A 3D convolution for video processing", "ans": "b", "exp": "Depthwise separable convolutions (used in MobileNet) reduce computation by ~8-9× vs standard convolutions with minimal accuracy loss, ideal for mobile deployment."},
        {"q": "What is global average pooling (GAP) and why is it preferred over flattening in some architectures?", "a": "Averaging across the batch dimension", "b": "Computing the average of each entire feature map to produce a single value per channel, reducing parameters and acting as a structural regularizer", "c": "A type of max pooling with global coverage", "d": "Averaging across channels", "ans": "b", "exp": "GAP replaces large fully-connected layers with a single average per feature map. It reduces parameters dramatically and is more interpretable (class activation maps)."},
